  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sh. Ajay Gupta vs State on 30 October, 2018 In The Court Of Ms. Poonam A. Bamba District & Sessions Judge, New Delhi In the matters of : Fsat No. 6/16 1. Sh. Ajay Gupta S/o Late Sh. Ram Babu Gupta R/o Kewal Park, Azadpur, Delhi. 2. Smt. Shashi Bala Gupta W/o Late Sh.Ram Babu Gupta R/o Kewal Park, Azadpur, Delhi. Versus 1. State, Through Commissioner Department of Food Safety Govt. of Nct of Delhi A20, Lawrence Road Delhi110035. 2. Designated Officer Department of Food Safety Govt. of Nct of Delhi A20, Lawrence Road Delhi110035 . 3. Sh.Saurav Sharma Food Safety Officer Department of Food Safety Govt. of Nct of Delhi A20, Lawrence Road Delhi110035. Fsat No.6/16 Ajay Gupta & Anr. Vs Fso Appeal Against The Order Dated 09.07.2014 Of Ld. Adjudicating Officer/ Additional District Magistrate(North), Delhi. Date of filing of appeal : 08.",
    "facts": "2.0 Facts in brief as per the appeal are that : i) on 02.07.2013 at about 4:00pm, Food Safety Officer FSAT No.6/16 Ajay Gupta & Anr. Vs FSO (\"FSO\" in short), purchased the sample8 sealed packets of 250 grams each of \"Mater Masala\", from Sh.Ajay Gupta S/o Late Shri Ram Babu Gupta at the premises of M/s Rajasthan Bhujia Bhandar,F14/19, Model TownII, Delhi110009; (ii) two sealed packets of the sample food commodity constituted one sample counterpart. One such sealed sample was sent to the Food Analyst on 03.07.2013; iii) the Food Analyst vide his report dated 15.07.2013 reported the sample to be misbranded; iv) the Food Safety Officer filed a complaint/application under Rule 3.1.1(3) FSS Rules, 2011 before the Ld. ADM alleging violation of Section 26(2)(ii) & Section 27(1) of FSS Act and Regulations no.2.2.2.1, 2.2.2.4, 2.2.2.8, 2.2.2.10, 2.3.1.5 & 2.3.2.1(a) FSS (P&L) Regulations, punishable under Sections 52 & 58 of FSS Act. The appellants have challenged the impugned order interalia on the grounds that : i) Ld. ADM failed to appreciate that the FSO did not follow the mandatory provisions of law. ii) Ld.ADM failed to appreciate that the rule 2.4.2(1) of FSS Rules, which requires that the Food Analyst shall compare the seals on the container and the outer cover with specimen impression of seal received separately and shall note the condition of the seal thereon, was not complied with by the Food Analyst. Thus, the Ld. ADM rightly passed the impugned order. This appeal is therefore,frivolous and deserves to be dismissed with heavy cost. It is also not in dispute that the Food Analyst vide his report dated 15.07.2013 reported the sample as misbranded as it violated Regulations 2.2.2.1, 2.2.2.4, 2.2.2.10, 2.3.1.5 & 2.3.2.1(a) of FSS(P&L)Regulation. 6.1 In view of the above, the Food Safety Officer filed a complaint/application under Rule 3.1.1(3) FSS Rules, 2011 before the Ld.ADM. Notice of the same was issued to the appellants. Perusal of the record shows that in response to the notice issued by Ld. ADM, Sh.Ajay Gupta, appellant no.1 put in appearance on 12.06.2014 and 25.06.2014. The proceedings of 25.06.2014, read as under \"25.06.2014 .....FBO approached & he stated that he is not aware of the provision of Food & Safety Act 2006 and he runs his business since long period. The officials for we measurement had informed for preparing package in present form & they have complied that instruction. However FBO pleaded guilty of the violation of FSS Act/Rule/Regulation ..... \" 6.2 From the above, it is evident that the appellant expressed ignorance about the provisions of FSS Act and pleaded guilty. None of the contentions as raised in the present appeal were raised before the Ld.ADM.",
    "arguments": "7.2 It is further contended by the appellants that the Ld.ADM failed to appreciate that the rule 2.4.2(1) of FSS Rules, which requires that the Food Analyst shall compare the seals on the container and the outer cover with specimen impression of seal FSAT No.6/16 Ajay Gupta & Anr. Vs FSO received separately and shall note the condition of the seal thereon, was not complied with by the Food Analyst.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "7.2 It is further contended by the appellants that the Ld.ADM failed to appreciate that the rule 2.4.2(1) of FSS Rules, which requires that the Food Analyst shall compare the seals on the container and the outer cover with specimen impression of seal FSAT No.6/16 Ajay Gupta & Anr. Vs FSO received separately and shall note the condition of the seal thereon, was not complied with by the Food Analyst.",
